From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: misc-de <misc-de@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:10:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] gesture: Don't let a moving window move the finger

A slow drag on the app grid of a phone shell made the list jump back
and forth by a whole row between two frames while the finger moved
steadily.

GDK converts a touch event to the coordinates of its window when the
event arrives, GtkGesture converts it back with the window's position
at the time it is handled. A scrolled GtkViewport moves its bin window
with each event, so when more events are waiting, every further one
contains the window's move as if the finger had made it. The scrolled
window follows that again, and the content oscillates. The same
happens to every gesture on a widget whose window moves under the
finger (libhandy's carousel showed it on the lock screen).

For touch events, take the root coordinates instead, which GDK computes
when the event arrives and which don't depend on any window, and
subtract the event widget window's current origin. Root coordinates
and window positions are whole pixels, so the fraction of the window
coordinates is kept.

Measured on a phone with a 240 Hz touch screen, recording the screen
and the raw touch positions: the finger moved 20 px per frame, the
grid of Phosh's overview jumped by -52, +52, -54 px and by whole rows
of 180 px in consecutive frames.
---
  gtk/gtkgesture.c | 37 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
  1 file changed, 37 insertions(+)

diff --git a/gtk/gtkgesture.c b/gtk/gtkgesture.c
index bfc8ce0..2d21673 100644
--- a/gtk/gtkgesture.c
+++ b/gtk/gtkgesture.c
@@ -465,6 +465,21 @@ _get_event_coordinates (PointData *data,
     *y = event_y;
 }
 
+static gboolean
+_gtk_gesture_event_is_touch (const GdkEvent *event)
+{
+  switch ((guint) event->type)
+    {
+    case GDK_TOUCH_BEGIN:
+    case GDK_TOUCH_UPDATE:
+    case GDK_TOUCH_END:
+    case GDK_TOUCH_CANCEL:
+      return TRUE;
+    default:
+      return FALSE;
+    }
+}
+
 static void
 _update_widget_coordinates (GtkGesture *gesture,
                             PointData  *data)
@@ -497,6 +512,28 @@ _update_widget_coordinates (GtkGesture *gesture,
   if (!window)
     return;
 
+  /* GDK gives a touch event the coordinates of its window when the event arrives, the loop above
+   * converts them back with the positions the windows have now. A scrolled viewport or a swiped
+   * page moves its window with each event, so with more events waiting, every further one would
+   * contain the move made for those before it as if the finger had made it, and the content
+   * jumps back and forth. Take the finger's place on the surface instead, it doesn't depend on
+   * any window. Root coordinates are whole pixels, as are window positions, so the fraction is
+   * the one of the window coordinates.
+   */
+  if (_gtk_gesture_event_is_touch (data->event))
+    {
+      gdouble root_x, root_y;
+      gint origin_x, origin_y;
+
+      if (gdk_event_get_root_coords (data->event, &root_x, &root_y))
+        {
+          _get_event_coordinates (data, &event_x, &event_y);
+          gdk_window_get_root_coords (event_widget_window, 0, 0, &origin_x, &origin_y);
+          event_x = floor (root_x) - origin_x + (event_x - floor (event_x));
+          event_y = floor (root_y) - origin_y + (event_y - floor (event_y));
+        }
+    }
+
   if (!gtk_widget_get_has_window (event_widget))
     {
       gtk_widget_get_allocation (event_widget, &allocation);
